<LegalLayout
  title="Acceptable Use Policy"
  description="The narrow set of things you may not do with Merkleye or with what it finds."
  updated="[EFFECTIVE DATE]"
>
  <p>
    This policy is part of the <a href="/legal/terms/">Terms of Service</a> and
    the <a href="/legal/license/">Commercial License Agreement</a>. It is
    deliberately short. Merkleye reads a public, append-only log; monitoring
    Certificate Transparency is not something we intend to police.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What is explicitly fine</h2>
  <p>
    So the boundary is clear, none of the following needs permission from us:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Monitoring domains you do not own. CT logs are public by design, and
      watching a competitor&rsquo;s or a supplier&rsquo;s certificate issuance
      is ordinary threat intelligence.
    </li>
    <li>
      Generating and monitoring lookalike permutations of any brand, including
      ones that are not yours.
    </li>
    <li>Security research, teaching, and publishing what you find.</li>
    <li>
      Running Merkleye for clients as a service provider, subject to a licence
      that covers those domains.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. What you may not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Use Merkleye or its output to attack.</strong> Findings must not
      be used to select targets for unauthorised access, to register the
      lookalike domains it surfaces for your own phishing, or to facilitate
      fraud.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Target individuals.</strong> Do not use Merkleye to monitor,
      profile, stalk, or harass a natural person, or to build a surveillance
      product aimed at private individuals.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Suppress lawful speech.</strong> Do not use findings to support
      takedown or abuse complaints you know to be unfounded, against criticism,
      parody, or legitimate research.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Abuse third-party infrastructure.</strong> Do not configure
      Merkleye to place unreasonable load on CT log operators, DNS resolvers,
      or any API, or to evade their rate limits or terms.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Evade licensing.</strong> Do not chain evaluation licences,
      misreport domain counts, or split one deployment across entities to stay
      under a cap. See the <a href="/legal/license/">licence</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Break the law.</strong> Including export controls and sanctions.
      We do not knowingly license to parties on a restricted-party list or in a
      comprehensively sanctioned jurisdiction.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misuse this website.</strong> No attempts to gain unauthorised
      access, no denial-of-service, no scraping that degrades it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Consequences</h2>
  <p>
    For the AGPL-licensed software, we have no technical means of enforcement
    and do not want any — there is no remote kill switch and no phone-home. Our
    remedies are contractual: we may suspend or terminate a commercial licence,
    decline to renew, refuse support, and where the conduct is serious, report
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Reporting</h2>
  <p>
    Report abuse to <a href="mailto:abuse@merkleye.com">abuse@merkleye.com</a>.
    For vulnerabilities in Merkleye itself, use the
    <a href="/legal/security/">disclosure process</a> instead.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
